Both requests succeeded. Let me read the full responses to get all the data.

Vehicle positions response:
```json
{
  "vehicles": [
    {"vehicle_id": "veh-4471", "route_id": "RT-12", "lat": 33.7561, "lon": -84.3902, "bearing": 178},
    {"vehicle_id": "veh-4490", "route_id": "RT-19", "lat": 33.772, "lon": -84.3631, "bearing": 92}
  ],
  "active_count": 2
}
```


Trip updates response:
```json
{
  "trip_updates": [
    {"trip_id": "trip-88231", "route_id": "RT-12", "delay_seconds": 340},
    {"trip_id": "trip-88240", "route_id": "RT-19", "delay_seconds": 60}
  ]
}
```

So I've got two active vehicles covering routes RT-12 and RT-19, both with trip updates available. Now I need to check if there are any routes that don't have corresponding trip updates.

Looking at the vehicle routes against what's in the trip updates, every active route has a match—there are no gaps between what's running and what's being tracked. Both routes RT-12 and RT-19 show up in the trip updates with their delays, so there are zero uncovered active vehicles out of the two total.
